Beginning with my motivation for learning high-performance computing, I feel like using shell and coding through the terminal has been intimidating to me in the past. We worked in the terminal briefly in early computer science classes, and I was never really sure that I was doing it correctly or understanding the best way to use it since we were mostly just running files from VSCode. So, I’m excited to gain more of an understanding of coding and working with data this way, and I can already say that it’s less intimidating than I thought! Additionally, I am doing an IT/data science internship for an insurance company over the summer and graduating soon, and did something similar at a different company last summer. In my previous internship, I worked with third party data / internal location data a lot, and it was stressed over and over again how important reproducibility and clarity are when working with a project involving so much data and preparation of data. So, being able to work on these skills in a class setting with real data and learn more about managing data pipelines is a skill that I think is really important to learn/improve upon as well. Last summer I also worked with a remote desktop (briefly) for the first time, and I think it would be helpful to get some more experience with that as well as something I have seen in a real world setting.

My goals for this course are similar to what I mentioned above: I want to feel comfortable working in the terminal and gain knowledge of another skillset that can be used in data science. I also think it is really exciting to learn the bioinformatics side of this class- my sister and a few of my friends are biology majors and I'm really excited to learn something that is related to what they are learning! Overall I really want to be able to confidently interact with HPC and shell command coding, so I am excited to learn as much as possible!

As for plan of attack- for this first assignment, I have been trying to accomplish as much as I can based on lectures, and then complete the practices as well. In completing practice one I did have to go back and look in the lessons or on the internet for some of the commands, but I started a document that I intend to copy into my supercomputing folder that lists the commands so that I can access/remember how to do what we have learned without googling if possible. The practice was really helpful and I’m glad to have the extra resource.

When creating the project folder for this assignment, I followed from the reading to include separate folders for data, scripts, and outputs as well as a “docs” folder. When I was working over the summer on my machine learning project for my internship, documentation was extremely important. Because of this, I wanted to make sure I have a folder set as an organized place to put these documents that is separate from the data/scripts. Having these four folders ensures that everything is separate and can be updated independently- from the lesson and reading it is really important that outputs need to be replaceable and everything else documented.
